<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julio C. Tello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Julio César Tello</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julio C. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Tello</a> was a Peruvian archaeologist, often called the father of Peruvian archaeology, who argued that Andean civilisation developed locally. He studied Chavín de Huántar, and in 1925 discovered the Paracas cemeteries on the south coast, excavating the necropolis of Wari Kayan with hundreds of mummy bundles wrapped in embroidered textiles. He founded museums of archaeology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julio-c-tello.jpg" alt="Julio C. Tello" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julio_C._Tello_Harvard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
